Building a Farcaster-Native Application
Farcaster is a decentralized social protocol built around permissionless identity, signed messages, and an open event graph. Developing an application on Farcaster involves interacting with its core components:

Hubs – Distributed nodes that store and serve Farcaster messages with cryptographic integrity guarantees.

Frames – Rich, interactive UI surfaces rendered directly inside Farcaster clients, enabling on-chain or off-chain actions with minimal friction.

Farcaster API / Neynar API – Interfaces for querying users, casts, reactions, and writing signed messages to the network.

Signer Authorization – A delegated key system that allows applications to post on behalf of users securely.

By integrating these components, developers can implement features such as cast publishing, real-time social feeds, identity-linked transactions, and on-chain data workflows.
This repository showcases how to authenticate users, interact with Hubs, validate message signatures, and build Frame-enabled endpoints for interactive experiences.

Farcaster’s fully open architecture allows you to build modular, scalable, and user-owned social applications without centralized dependencies